#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221125 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48cd517d-be8a-5dc2-91af-fbd19231363c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing GTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/id #uuid "fb9baafb-4eca-593f-ad3e-34744fdfd3f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21684" :question/problem "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $\\Delta x$ is\n$e(\\Delta x) = 4(\\Delta x)^3 + (\\Delta x)^4$.\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\quad$ With step size $\\Delta x = 0.4$, the global truncation error is $XXX$\n\n$\\quad$ With step size $\\Delta x = 0.2$, the global truncation error is $XXX$\n\n$\\quad$ Decreasing from $\\Delta x = 0.4$ to $\\Delta x = 0.2$, the GTE is reduced by a factor of $XXX$\n\n$\\quad$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n$XXX$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^p\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately $k^p$.\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.4$, the GTE is\n$e(0.4)|= 4(0.4)^{3} + (0.4)^{4}; = 4 \\cdot 0.064 + 1 \\cdot 0.0256; = 0.256 + 0.0256; = 0.2816; \\approx 0.282$.\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.2$, the GTE is\n$e(0.2)|= 4(0.2)^{3} + (0.2)^{4}; = 4 \\cdot 0.008 + 1 \\cdot 0.0016; = 0.032 + 0.0016; = 0.0336; \\approx 0.034$.\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.2$. So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{0.4}{0.2} = 2\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p$, the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n$k^p = 2^p$.\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.281\\,6$ to $0.033\\,6$. So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.281\\,6}{0.033\\,6} = 8.380\\,95\\ldots \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{8.381}\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2^{p} & \\approx 8.380952… \\\\ p & \\approx \\log_{2} (8.38095…) \\\\ p & \\approx 3.067…\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gray]{3}$.\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = 4(\\Delta x)^3 + (\\Delta x)^4 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad e(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^3\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x\\to 0\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221126 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/id #uuid "c94bf874-2055-5ff3-83e3-b5740a1e14e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-21685" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.24$, the observed local truncation error is $\\tau(0.24) = 0.8$.\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.08$, the observed local truncation error is $\\tau(0.08) = 0.01$.\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method, rounded to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately $k^{p+1}$.\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.24$ to $0.08$. So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{0.24}{0.08} = 3\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p$, the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\nk^{p+1} = 3^{p+1}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.8$ to $0.01$. So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.8}{0.01} = 80\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3^{p + 1} & \\approx 80 \\\\ p + 1 & \\approx \\log_{3} (80) \\\\ p + 1 & \\approx 3.987… \\\\ p & \\approx 2.987…\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221127 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/id #uuid "b9b0118d-5238-5644-a3d6-695d8f393efe" :question/math-academy-id "e-21686" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($\\tau$) represent a second-order numerical method?\n\n1. $e(\\Delta x) = 3(\\Delta x)^2 + 4(\\Delta x)^5$\n2. $\\tau(\\Delta x) = 0.6(\\Delta x)^3 + 2(\\Delta x)^4$\n3. $e(\\Delta x) = 7(\\Delta x)^4 + 5(\\Delta x)^6$" :question/worked-solution "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n$e(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O((\\Delta x)^p)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0$,\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n$\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O((\\Delta x)^{p+1})\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0$,\n\nthen the method is of order $p$.\n\nWith that in mind, let’s check each option:\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(\\Delta x) = 3(\\Delta x)^2 + 4(\\Delta x)^5$, the dominant term as $\\Delta x\\to 0$ is $3(\\Delta x)^2 = 3(\\Delta x)^2$.\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n- For the LTE expression $\\tau(\\Delta x) = 0.6(\\Delta x)^3 + 2(\\Delta x)^4$, the dominant term as $\\Delta x\\to 0$ is $0.6(\\Delta x)^3 = 0.6(\\Delta x)^{2+1}$.\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n- For the GTE expression $e(\\Delta x) = 7(\\Delta x)^4 + 5(\\Delta x)^6$, the dominant term as $\\Delta x\\to 0$ is $7(\\Delta x)^4 = 7(\\Delta x)^4$.\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221128 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4c7f962-87af-53af-8167-d24ade127366" :knowledge-point/title "Making Inferences Given the Order of a Numerical Method"}] :question/id #uuid "de3c4be6-9d3d-5dcb-b1cf-567195c4ee63" :question/math-academy-id "e-21687" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $1$.\nSuppose that using step size $\\Delta x = 0.4$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $\\tau(0.4) \\approx 0.24$.\nIf we instead use a step size $\\Delta x = 0.1$, what LTE should we expect?\n\n$XXX$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)\\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately $k^{p+1}$.\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.1$. So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\frac{0.4}{0.1} = 4\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $1$, the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\nk^{1+1} = 4^2 = 16\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 0.1\n$$\n\nthe expected LTE is\n\n$$\n\\tau(0.1) \\approx \\frac{0.24}{16} = 0.015\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a064cf27-b75b-5e43-96b0-efebe92e7479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220460} {:answer/id #uuid "d3079631-4123-5f02-9f03-b26206188e15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220461} {:answer/id #uuid "134ee36b-8546-51cd-bbfc-fbfae2b3c95c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220462} {:answer/id #uuid "7b63cdd9-58f9-51f7-8bf8-5a3c43519907" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220463} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6a0180-06ef-53b7-9e1d-4662ba5050a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c6a0180-06ef-53b7-9e1d-4662ba5050a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220464} :answer-field/id #uuid "87eafb71-b874-5755-aae9-466ade2be33e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220459}] :question/id #uuid "a3f43810-818f-5e05-945e-483e5e28efaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-333692" :question/problem "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a second-order numerical method?\n\n1. $e(Δx) = 6(Δx)^{2}$\n2. $e(Δx) = 0.3Δx + 2(Δx)^{2}$\n3. $e(Δx) = 9(Δx)^{2} + 4(Δx)^{5}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87d09fa3-8fdd-5038-ace0-31a77352a565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220474} {:answer/id #uuid "964c7a81-6fd2-5ae9-957f-09f9caa2091e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220475} {:answer/id #uuid "b213d399-1c34-5729-a1bf-3f21bb602155" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220476} {:answer/id #uuid "10ca0000-30fa-55dc-a19c-26afb00d8517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220477} {:answer/id #uuid "51e03494-beb8-5d27-9de5-24b4756fe64f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "964c7a81-6fd2-5ae9-957f-09f9caa2091e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220475} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a369baf-f92b-5790-b811-839f7e9b4e20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220473}] :question/id #uuid "07099fdd-1348-5e3a-a68e-3d7b94b8ddb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-333702" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx = 0.08$, the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.08) = 0.13$.\n- With step size $Δx = 0.02$, the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.02) = 0.002$.\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 1759218622